WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Anyone who's ever played Monopoly knows how the typical game goes:

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.280
First, a scramble for property.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.820
It's a giant game of Hungry Hungry Hippos;

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.660
Roll. Buy. Repeat.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.940
Next, there's a lull;

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.300
No monopolies, no trading, nothing to do.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.600
Eventually, someone gets a monopoly, and if they have half a brain,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.320
they build houses, collect rent, and build more houses.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.200
In a good game, there's some competition,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.300
But even if more than one player has a monopoly,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.140
it's still just a waiting game of "Who's Property Will Be Landed On First"?

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.980
And if there's only one monopoly, it's much worse.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.220
Either way, a slow, painful death begins for all but one player.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.600
The rest of the game can an hour or six,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.200
But it's just Monopoly Theater;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.700
Continuing to play even though the winner is obvious.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.440
Now, compare that with the simplest game there is:

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.000
Uno.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.340
Play a matching card, and pick up more if you can't.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.340
The goal is simple: Get rid of your cards.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.900
As you get closer to winning, you have fewer cards in your hand, making matches less likely.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.040
This naturally balances the game, even with luck involved.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.140
If this is Monopoly: Stale and Boring,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.880
This is Uno: Up and Down, always uncertain.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.820
Make a list of Monpoly's flaws, and you'll realize just how bad it is:

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.860
It's mostly luck based, there's lots of waiting on other players,

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.020
there's a big lull before monopolies, little trading ever happens,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.940
balance is rare, and the outcome is clear early on.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.360
So if you dislike Monopoly, I can't blame you.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.920
But I think the amount of hate it gets is unfair.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.120
But hold that for a second;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.760
Let's talk about Papers, Please.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.500
This is a game where you work as a passport inspector for a dystopian government.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.700
You deny entry to helpless immigrants, separate families, and lie,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:28.960
all to feed your family.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.540
Now I love this game, but it's not fun.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.120
Most of your time is spent looking at boring documents.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.500
Make a tiny mistake, and you get a citation,

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.360
which means no food for your family.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.500
Even its biggest fans find it nerve-wracking and stressful.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.220
But we love it because we know that every annoyance was designed to teach us something.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.440
It's an experience.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.160
Now, Monopoly is much more like Papers, Please:

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.680
It's not fun, but it is good.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.080
Everything you dislike was designed to make you feel capitalism.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.580
The ridiculously unfair power of a monopoly is purposeful and the emphasis on luck is no mistake.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.040
If it were fun, it wouldn't really be meaningful.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.680
Hating Monopoly is like hating 12 Years A Slave because it wasn't enjoyable to watch.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.620
That's not really fair. You're using the wrong criteria.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.600
So if Papers, Please and Monopoly are so similar,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.700
why is one hated and the other loved?

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.300
I'd blame marketing.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.200
Monopoly is advertised as "Family Fun", setting the wrong expectations.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.720
Papers, Please knows what it is: Art

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.740
When you get frustrated playing Monopoly, you're looking at the box thinking,

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.160
"You lied; this isn't fun."

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.680
Monopoly lets you experience its message first-hand,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:32.800
which is pretty neat,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.640
but it should be advertised as such,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.560
not as a family game like Uno.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.900
A university professor actually tried to correct for some of these flaws

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.860
with what he called "Anti-Monopoly";  his own take on the game.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.660
And when Parker Brothers sued him for copyright infringement,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.800
they further proved the critique on capitalism of their own game.

